ame out in 2013, it will take some time for people to recognize a different=
 mental model and teach it.</strong></p>
<hr>
<h2>TLDR</h2>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s a quick TLDR if you don=E2=80=99t want to read the whole =
thing. If some parts don=E2=80=99t make sense, you can scroll down until yo=
u find something related.</p>
<p>Feel free to skip it if you plan to read the whole post. I=E2=80=99ll li=
nk to it at the end.</p>
<p><strong>=F0=9F=A4=94 Question: How do I replicate <code>componentDidMoun=
t</code> with <code>useEffect</code>?</strong></p>
<p>While you can <code>useEffect(fn, [])</code>, it=E2=80=99s not an exact =
equivalent. Unlike <code>componentDidMount</code>, it will <em>capture</em>=
 props and state. So even inside the callbacks, you=E2=80=99ll see the init=
ial props and state. If you want to see =E2=80=9Clatest=E2=80=9D something,=
 you can write it to a ref. But there=E2=80=99s usually a simpler way to st=
ructure the code so that you don=E2=80=99t have to. Keep in mind that the m=
ental model for effects is different from <code>componentDidMount</code> an=
d other lifecycles, and trying to find their exact equivalents may confuse =
you more than help. To get productive, you need to =E2=80=9Cthink in effect=
s=E2=80=9D, and their mental model is closer to implementing synchronizatio=
n than to responding to lifecycle events.</p>
<p><strong>=F0=9F=A4=94 Question:  How do I correctly fetch data inside <co=
de>useEffect</code>? What is <code>[]</code>?</strong></p>
<p><a target=3D"_blank" class=3D" scale-100 active:scale-100" style=3D"opac=
ity:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-in-out, opacity 0.2s 0.4s linear" href=
=3D"https://www.robinwieruch.de/react-hooks-fetch-data/">This article</a> i=
s a good primer on data fetching with <code>useEffect</code>. Make sure to =
read it to the end! It=E2=80=99s not as long as this one. <code>[]</code> m=
eans the effect doesn=E2=80=99t use any value that participates in React da=
ta flow, and is for that reason safe to apply once. It is also a common sou=
rce of bugs when the value actually <em>is</em> used. You=E2=80=99ll need t=
o learn a few strategies (primarily <code>useReducer</code> and <code>useCa=
llback</code>) that can <em>remove the need</em> for a dependency instead o=
f incorrectly omitting it.</p>
<p><strong>=F0=9F=A4=94 Question: Do I need to specify functions as effect =
dependencies or not?</strong></p>
<p>The recommendation is to hoist functions that don=E2=80=99t need props o=
r state <em>outside</em> of your component, and pull the ones that are used=
 only by an effect <em>inside</em> of that effect.  If after that your effe=
ct still ends up using functions in the render scope (including function fr=
om props), wrap them into <code>useCallback</code> where they=E2=80=99re de=
fined, and repeat the process. Why does it matter? Functions can =E2=80=9Cs=
ee=E2=80=9D values from props and state =E2=80=94 so they participate in th=
e data flow. There=E2=80=99s a <a target=3D"_blank" class=3D" scale-100 act=
ive:scale-100" style=3D"opacity:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-in-out, op=
acity 0.2s 0.4s linear" href=3D"https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-faq.html#is-=
it-safe-to-omit-functions-from-the-list-of-dependencies">more detailed answ=
er</a> in our FAQ.</p>
<p><strong>=F0=9F=A4=94 Question: Why do I sometimes get an infinite refetc=
hing loop?</strong></p>
<p>This can happen if you=E2=80=99re doing data fetching in an effect witho=
ut the second dependencies argument. Without it, effects run after every re=
nder =E2=80=94 and setting the state will trigger the effects again. An inf=
inite loop may also happen if you specify a value that <em>always</em> chan=
ges in the dependency array. You can tell which one by removing them one by=
 one. However, removing a dependency you use (or blindly specifying <code>[=
]</code>) is usually the wrong fix. Instead, fix the problem at its source.=
 For example, functions can cause this problem, and putting them inside eff=
ects, hoisting them out, or wrapping them with <code>useCallback</code> hel=
ps. To avoid recreating objects, <code>useMemo</code> can serve a similar p=
urpose.</p>
<p><strong>=F0=9F=A4=94 Why do I sometimes get an old state or prop value i=
nside my effect?</strong></p>
<p>Effects always =E2=80=9Csee=E2=80=9D props and state from the render the=
y were defined in. That <a class=3D" scale-100 active:scale-100" style=3D"o=
pacity:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-in-out, opacity 0.2s 0.4s linear" h=
ref=3D"https://overreacted.io/how-are-function-components-different-from-cl=
asses/">helps prevent bugs</a> but in some cases can be annoying. For those=
 cases, you can explicitly maintain some value in a mutable ref (the linked=
 article explains it at the end). If you think you=E2=80=99re seeing some p=
rops or state from an old render but don=E2=80=99t expect it, you probably =
missed some dependencies. Try using the <a target=3D"_blank" class=3D" scal=
e-100 active:scale-100" style=3D"opacity:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-i=
n-out, opacity 0.2s 0.4s linear" href=3D"https://github.com/facebook/react/=
issues/14920">lint rule</a> to train yourself to see them. A few days, and =
it=E2=80=99ll be like a second nature to you. See also <a target=3D"_blank"=
 class=3D" scale-100 active:scale-100" style=3D"opacity:1;transition:transf=
orm 0.2s ease-in-out, opacity 0.2s 0.4s linear" href=3D"https://reactjs.org=
/docs/hooks-faq.html#why-am-i-seeing-stale-props-or-state-inside-my-functio=
n">this answer</a> in our FAQ.</p>
<hr>
<p>I hope this TLDR was helpful! Otherwise, let=E2=80=99s go.</p>
