rent <code>count</code>. <strong>All we needed to tell React is to incremen=
t the state =E2=80=94 whatever it is right now.</strong></p>
<p>That=E2=80=99s exactly what <code>setCount(c =3D&gt; c + 1)</code> does.=
 You can think of it as =E2=80=9Csending an instruction=E2=80=9D to React a=
bout how the state should change. This =E2=80=9Cupdater form=E2=80=9D also =
helps in other cases, like when you <a class=3D" scale-100 active:scale-100=
" style=3D"opacity:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-in-out, opacity 0.2s 0.=
4s linear" href=3D"https://overreacted.io/react-as-a-ui-runtime/#batching">=
batch multiple updates</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Note that we actually <em>did the work</em> to remove the depend=
ency. We didn=E2=80=99t cheat. Our effect doesn=E2=80=99t read the <code>co=
unter</code> value from the render scope anymore:</strong></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://overreacted.io/a-complete-guide-to-useeffect/interva=
l-right.gif" alt=3D"Diagram of interval that works"></p>
