<p><strong>I want to emphasize that putting <code>useCallback</code> everyw=
here is pretty clunky.</strong> It=E2=80=99s a nice escape hatch and it=E2=
=80=99s useful when a function is both passed down <em>and</em> called from=
 inside an effect in some children. Or if you=E2=80=99re trying to prevent =
breaking memoization of a child component. But Hooks lend themselves better=
 to <a target=3D"_blank" class=3D" scale-100 active:scale-100" style=3D"opa=
city:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-in-out, opacity 0.2s 0.4s linear" hre=
f=3D"https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-faq.html#how-to-avoid-passing-callbacks=
-down">avoiding passing callbacks down</a> altogether.</p>
<p>In the above examples, I=E2=80=99d much prefer if <code>fetchData</code>=
 was either inside my effect (which itself could be extracted to a custom H=
ook) or a top-level import. I want to keep the effects simple, and callback=
s in them don=E2=80=99t help that. (=E2=80=9CWhat if some <code>props.onCom=
plete</code> callback changes while the request was in flight?=E2=80=9D) Yo=
u can <a target=3D"_blank" class=3D" scale-100 active:scale-100" style=3D"o=
pacity:1;transition:transform 0.2s ease-in-out, opacity 0.2s 0.4s linear" h=
ref=3D"https://overreacted.io/a-complete-guide-to-useeffect/#swimming-again=
st-the-tide">simulate the class behavior</a> but that doesn=E2=80=99t solve=
 race conditions.</p>
